00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:46.640
Most consecutive stairs climbed

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.460
while balancing a person on the head.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:50.910
Who set this record in the first place?

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:54.180
Wow. I have trouble doing a handstand.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.600
These guys are headstanding up steps.

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:59.300
Well deserved. And look
at the beautiful location.

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:00.550
This should be a double award.

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.540
Most beautiful backdrop

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:05.410
for consecutive stairs climbed
with a person on the head.

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:06.373
I wonder how long they practice for.

00:10:06.373 --> 00:10:09.240
Oh my God, can you
imagine if they mess up?

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:12.730
You fall down all those steps. Worst fear.

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:15.966
(suspenseful classical music)

00:10:15.966 --> 00:10:17.140
What is this a contest for?

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:20.189
Albert Einstein lookalikes? Hah.

00:10:20.189 --> 00:10:24.548
(man speaking foreign language)

00:10:24.548 --> 00:10:26.470
Oh, are these hyper smellers?

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:28.436
It's, like, hyperosmia or something.

00:10:28.436 --> 00:10:32.610
(man speaking foreign language)

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:33.480
- It's really true, though.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:35.660
There are some people who are
considered super-smellers.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:37.820
In fact, we did a whole
special episode tailored

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:39.970
to an individual who was a super-smeller

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:43.590
and can actually smell
Parkinson's on certain patients,

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:44.930
including her own husband.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:47.920
So remember, the sense of
smell is a chemical receptor.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.200
It's when a chemical binds

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.410
to the olfactory epithelium
within your nose,

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.500
and it starts sending
nerve signals to your brain

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:56.490
in order for you to understand
what that chemical is.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:11:00.060
The longest nose on a living
person belongs to this person.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:03.040
Just because his nose is long doesn't mean

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:05.570
that's why he has such
a great sense of smell.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.570
Remember, this is a neurological
phenomenon that's happening

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:12.640
but I will say you can have
disturbances in your anatomy.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:14.433
The olfactory cleft can be bigger

00:11:14.433 --> 00:11:17.350
or smaller in some people
or blocked off in others,

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:18.880
where it could decide whether

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:20.670
or not you have a strong sense of smell.

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:23.180
The olfactory region in dogs in comparison

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:24.740
to humans is so much better.

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:27.680
Like 40,000 times better.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:29.920
